\lispPackageChapter{candlesticks/durations}

A duration is one candlestick time window: a short name (such as
\texttt{"w"}), a long name (such as \texttt{"week"}), and the PostgreSQL
interval that corresponds to it.  The conventional set is second, minute,
hour, day, week, month, and year.

\section{Types}

\lispTypeSubsection{duration}

A CLOS class with slots \texttt{id}, \texttt{short-name},
\texttt{long-name}, and \texttt{interval}.  The accessors are
\lispId{duration-id}, \lispId{duration-short-name},
\lispId{duration-long-name}, and \lispId{duration-interval}.
\texttt{durationp} is true of instances.

\section{Functions}

\lispFunctionSubsection{standard-durations}

The conventional \texttt{(short-name long-name interval)} triples.  A
fresh copy is returned, so callers may mutate the result freely.

\lispFunctionSubsection{make-duration}

Ensure a duration with the given short name, long name, and PostgreSQL
interval exists, and return it.  Short names are unique on their own, and
so are long names --- there is only one \texttt{"d"} and only one
\texttt{"week"}.  If those two names already belong to different rows,
that is an error.

\lispSyntax

\lispCall{\texttt{(make-duration \lispVar{short-name} \lispVar{long-name} \lispVar{interval})}}

\lispFunctionSubsection{duration-by-short-name}

The duration with the given short name, or \texttt{nil}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{duration-by-long-name}

The duration with the given long name, or \texttt{nil}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{all-durations}

Every duration, in short-name order.

\lispFunctionSubsection{ensure-standard-durations}

Insert the standard second / minute / hour / day / week / month / year
durations if they are not already present, and return them.  Call this
once after the migrations have been applied.

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(ensure-standard-durations)
(duration-short-name (duration-by-long-name "week"))
; => "w"
\end{lstlisting}
